Q: How to split regular expression in PHP? A: <p><code>preg_match()</code> and <code>preg_split()</code> generate arrays, but you only want a string-type value. For that reason, <code>preg_replace()</code> provides a more direct/lean solution. <a href="https://3v4l.org/oIFPV#v" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<pre><code>var_export(
    preg_replace(
        '#.*?(\d+)\.aspx$#',
        '$1',
        'http://ebook.com/php+in+100+days-1088.aspx'
    )
);
</code></pre>
<p>If the URL only has the one hyphen and it is before the targeted number, then using <code>sscanf()</code> can declare an int-type variable holding the sought number. The <code>*</code> on the first placeholder prevents capturing the matched substring. <a href="https://3v4l.org/9ECsb#v" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>sscanf(
    'http://ebook.com/php+in+100+days-1088.aspx',
    '%*[^-]-%d',
    $number
);
var_export($number);
</code></pre>

Q: How to set consistent font fallback for mixed English/Hindi text in PptxGenJS? A: <p>Short answer: this is a real OOXML capability, but PptxGenJS's public API doesn't expose it, so in practice you have two workable options — split into runs (easy, works today) or patch the XML after generation (more elegant, handles arbitrary mixed strings).</p>
<h3>Why a single <code>fontFace</code> doesn't work</h3>
<p>OOXML text runs (<code>&lt;a:rPr&gt;</code>) actually support <strong>three separate typeface slots</strong>, not one:</p>
<p>xml</p>
<pre><code>&lt;a:rPr lang=&quot;en-US&quot;&gt;
  &lt;a:latin typeface=&quot;Arial&quot;/&gt;
  &lt;a:ea typeface=&quot;...&quot;/&gt;      &lt;!-- East Asian (CJK) --&gt;
  &lt;a:cs typeface=&quot;Nirmala UI&quot;/&gt; &lt;!-- Complex Script: Devanagari, Arabic, Hebrew, Thai, etc. --&gt;
&lt;/a:rPr&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>PowerPoint's renderer does per-character Unicode-range detection <strong>within a single run</strong> and picks <code>latin</code> or <code>cs</code> accordingly. So technically, OOXML lets you set both typefaces on <em>one</em> run and PowerPoint will render Latin glyphs with <code>latin</code> and Devanagari glyphs with <code>cs</code> automatically — no manual run-splitting required at the format level.</p>
<p>The catch: PptxGenJS's <code>fontFace</code> option only ever writes <code>&lt;a:latin&gt;</code>. It has no option for <code>cs</code> or <code>ea</code>. So when you set <code>fontFace: &quot;Arial&quot;</code>, the <code>cs</code> slot is left empty, and PowerPoint falls back to whatever the <strong>theme's minor/major complex-script font</strong> is — which differs by template, locale, and OS default, which is exactly the inconsistency you're seeing. Setting <code>fontFace: &quot;Nirmala UI&quot;</code> sets <code>latin</code> to Nirmala UI too, which is why your Latin text looks different from the rest of the deck.</p>
<p>So: not a PowerPoint/OOXML limitation — a PptxGenJS API gap.</p>
<h3>Option A — Split into runs (works today, no extra tooling)</h3>
<p>This is the officially-supported path for pptxgenjs, since <code>addText</code> accepts an array of <code>{text, options}</code> runs:</p>
<p>js</p>
<pre><code>const DEVANAGARI_RE = /[\u0900-\u097F]/;

function splitByScript(str) {
  const runs = [];
  let current = &quot;&quot;;
  let currentIsDevanagari = null;

  for (const ch of str) {
    const isDevanagari = DEVANAGARI_RE.test(ch);
    // Keep whitespace/punctuation attached to the current run
    const isNeutral = /\s|[/.,\-–—()]/.test(ch);

    if (currentIsDevanagari === null || isNeutral) {
      current += ch;
    } else if (isDevanagari === currentIsDevanagari) {
      current += ch;
    } else {
      runs.push({ text: current, isDevanagari: currentIsDevanagari });
      current = ch;
      currentIsDevanagari = isDevanagari;
    }
    if (currentIsDevanagari === null) currentIsDevanagari = isDevanagari;
  }
  if (current) runs.push({ text: current, isDevanagari: currentIsDevanagari });
  return runs;
}

function toPptxRuns(str, baseOpts) {
  return splitByScript(str).map(r =&gt; ({
    text: r.text,
    options: {
      ...baseOpts,
      fontFace: r.isDevanagari ? &quot;Nirmala UI&quot; : baseOpts.fontFace,
    },
  }));
}

slide.addText(
  toPptxRuns(&quot;Email Migration Guide / ईमेल माइग्रेशन गाइड&quot;, {
    fontSize: 24,
  }),
  { x: 0.5, y: 0.5, w: 9, h: 1 }
);
</code></pre>
<p>Notes:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><code>Nirmala UI</code> is the modern Windows Devanagari font (post-Win8); <code>Mangal</code> is the older/more universally-installed fallback. If you need max cross-platform coverage (older Windows, some Linux/macOS setups), you may want to pick <code>Mangal</code> since it's more consistently bundled, or offer it as the fallback after <code>Nirmala UI</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The neutral-character handling (spaces, <code>/</code>, punctuation) avoids fragmenting the string into dozens of tiny runs, which also avoids visible kerning/spacing seams between runs.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3>Option B — Post-process the XML to set <code>&lt;a:cs&gt;</code> directly (closer to &quot;true&quot; fallback)</h3>
<p>If you want the actual per-character auto-fallback behavior described above (useful when script boundaries are unpredictable, e.g. free-form user content), generate the pptx normally with a single run, then patch each run's <code>rPr</code> to add a <code>cs</code> typeface:</p>
<p>js</p>
<pre><code>const JSZip = require(&quot;jszip&quot;);
const fs = require(&quot;fs&quot;);

async function addComplexScriptFallback(pptxBuffer, csFont = &quot;Nirmala UI&quot;) {
  const zip = await JSZip.loadAsync(pptxBuffer);
  const slideFiles = Object.keys(zip.files).filter(f =&gt;
    /^ppt\/slides\/slide\d+\.xml$/.test(f)
  );

  for (const path of slideFiles) {
    let xml = await zip.file(path).async(&quot;string&quot;);
    // Add &lt;a:cs&gt; right after every &lt;a:latin .../&gt; that doesn't already have one
    xml = xml.replace(
      /(&lt;a:latin[^/]*\/&gt;)(?!\s*&lt;a:cs)/g,
      `$1&lt;a:cs typeface=&quot;${csFont}&quot;/&gt;`
    );
    zip.file(path, xml);
  }
  return zip.generateAsync({ type: &quot;nodebuffer&quot; });
}

// usage
const raw = await pptx.write(&quot;nodebuffer&quot;);
const patched = await addComplexScriptFallback(raw, &quot;Nirmala UI&quot;);
fs.writeFileSync(&quot;output.pptx&quot;, patched);
</code></pre>
<p>This keeps your original single <code>addText</code> call (<code>fontFace: &quot;Arial&quot;</code>) untouched, then injects the <code>cs</code> slot afterward. It's a bit more fragile (regex over XML), but it means you don't have to write script-detection/splitting logic yourself, and it correctly handles strings where scripts are interleaved in ways you didn't anticipate.</p>
<h3>Recommendation</h3>
<ul>
<li><p>If your bilingual strings are structured/predictable (like your title example, with a clear <code>/</code> separator), <strong>Option A (run-splitting)</strong> is simpler, robust, and doesn't touch generated XML — I'd default to this.</p>
</li>
<li><p>If you're generating decks from arbitrary/user-supplied mixed-script content where you can't reliably predict boundaries, <strong>Option B</strong> is worth the extra plumbing since it delegates the actual script-switch decision to PowerPoint's renderer rather than your own regex.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Either way, standardize on <strong>one specific Devanagari font</strong> (<code>Nirmala UI</code> or <code>Mangal</code>, not both, and not &quot;whatever's on this machine&quot;) and use it consistently across every run/slide — that's what actually gets you the &quot;renders the same regardless of which machine opens it&quot; property, since it removes the ambiguous per-system theme fallback entirely.</p>

Q: How to parse a formatted string with hyphens and square braces into a flat array of components A: <p>If no validation is needed, <code>preg_split()</code> can directly return the sought flat, 4-element array. Splits appear to occur on space-wrapped hyphens and the space-neighbored square braces. <a href="https://3v4l.org/lKAbI#v" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>var_export(
    preg_split('#]? +- +| +\[#', $string)
);
</code></pre>
<p>I might suggest <code>sscanf()</code> could be used for parsing a reliably formatted string with the ability to cast numbers as numeric types, but unfortunately the inner whitespacing before the opening square brace would need mopping up due to the functions greedy matching behavior. <a href="https://3v4l.org/qfa2h#v" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>var_export(
    sscanf($string, '%d - %[^[][%[^]]] - %f')
);
</code></pre>
<p>For validation and parsing if the string, <code>preg_match()</code> is ideal. I'll use a lookahead after matching the first component purely to avoid the unwanted full string match element and produce the exact desired payload. <a href="https://3v4l.org/1tHk6#v" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>preg_match(
    '#^\d+(?= - ([^\[]+?) +\[([^\]]+)] - (\d+\.\d+)$)#',
    $string,
    $m
);
var_export($m);
</code></pre>
